## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end CAPA design for a real multi-tenant data routing bug with data residency risk, ensuring traceability from detection to verification and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model with evidence, tenant IDs, and route metadata
- Region-aware lifecycle state machine with clear escalations
- Linked logs/traces in CAPA records for auditability
- Canary rollout and RCA templates with measurable metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal REST API skeleton for CAPA creation
app.post('/capa', (req, res) => {
  const { evidence, tenantId, routeInfo } = req.body;
  // persist CAPA and link logs/traces
  res.status(201).send({ id: 'capa-123', status: 'open' });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify false positives and tune thresholds across regions?
- What automated checks would prevent reintroduction of the misrouting in future deployments?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical CAPA program around data privacy, tenant isolation, and controlled policy changes in real-time fintech systems.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model: evidence, lineage, owners, policy references
- Tenant-aware lifecycle: detect, contain, remediate, verify with SLAs
- Minimal API: create/update CAPAs with logs/traces, policy links
- Metrics: containment time, recurrence rate, tenant impact
- RCA and canary rollback: structured **root cause** templates and safe rollout controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sketch: POST /api/capa
app.post('/api/capa', (req, res) => {
  const {evidence, lineage, owners, policyLink, tenantId} = req.body;
  // validate inputs, create CAPA, emit logs, assign owners
  res.status(201).json({capaId: 'CAPA-1234'});
